  "lineage": "Mexican son tradition centered on Jalisco-Nayarit highlands and codified through the early-20th-century studio era (1930s-1950s commercial recording boom) into the standardized mariachi-orquesta of two trumpets, three-to-six violins, vihuela rhythm-chord, guitarrón six-string acoustic-bass, and Spanish guitar. Derived from rural son jalisciense and son alteño fiddle-and-vihuela ensembles, transformed by 1907-onward Cocula-and-Tecalitlán municipal contests, professionalized via the 1925-onward Mexico City cantina circuit, and globalized through 1930s-onward Mexican golden-age cinema. The bolero-ranchera-and-son repertoire alternates 6/8-against-3/4 hemiola with vocally projected open-throat ranchera belting over instrumental triadic-and-harmonic-third doublings.",
